l structure the JSON accordingly.\n\n\"error_location\": \"def split_at_lowercase(s):\" (the function definition line with wrong name)\n\n\"error_reason\": \"Học sinh đã đặt tên hàm là 'split_at_lowercase' trong khi chương trình kiểm tra gọi hàm 'split_lowerstring', dẫn đến lỗi NameError: name 'split_lowerstring' is not defined. Bên cạnh đó, thuật toán của hàm không đúng với yêu cầu tách chuỗi tại vị trí chữ thường để mỗi phần tử bắt đầu bằng một chữ thường (theo test case).\"\n\nBut maybe simpler: just state the name mismatch as the root cause, and that the function needs to be named split_lowerstring. We'll include the logic fix in corrected_solution.\n\nNow for corrected_solution, I'll write:\n\ndef split_lowerstring(s):\n    result = []\n    current = \"\"\n    for char in s:\n        if char.islower():\n            if current:\n                result.append(current)\n            current = char\n        else:\n            current += char\n    if current:\n        result.append(current)\n    return result\n\nThat yields for \"AbCd\": 'A' not lower, current = \"A\"; 'b' lower, current non-empty, so append \"A\", current = \"b\"; 'C' not lower, current = \"bC\"; 'd' lower, current non-empty, append \"bC\", current = \"d\"; end, append \"d\". So result ['A', 'bC', 'd']? Wait, that logic: when lower, if current: result.append(current) then current = char. That would append \"A\" first, then current becomes \"b\". So result gets \"A\". That still includes leading uppercase. To fix, we need to not append the initial segment if we haven't started a lowercase-led segment. The correct logic: we only start building a segment when we hit a lowercase letter, and we only add to result when we finish a segment (i.e., when we hit a new lowercase or end). So we need a flag or just start current empty and only append when we have a lowercase. So:\n\ndef split_lowerstring(s):\n    result = []\n    current = \"\"\n    for char in s:\n        if char.islower():\n            if curre