So only pairs where num < n and partner may be > n, then it would add partner beyond n, wrong. The loop should probably filter to only sum numbers <= n. The student's code adds both numbers if a pair is found with num < n and partner may be > n, but they only add if partner > num (to avoid double counting). However, if partner > n, then it shouldn't be included. So there is a potential logical error: the set includes numbers that might exceed n. For the specific test case n=999, all amicable numbers up to 999 are: 220, 284, (1184,1210 not included because 1184>999). So it works. But if a test case had n such that a number m < n has an amicable partner p > n, that p would incorrectly be added. For instance, if n=3000, then all amicable pairs with both <=3000? Let's check: (220,284), (1184,1210), (2620,2924), (5020,5564) -> 5020 > 3000. So (2620,2924) both <3000, okay. But no issue. However, consider n=1200: then (220,284) both <1200, (1184,1210) 1184 <1200, partner 1210 >1200. The loop goes up to 1199, so num=1184, partner=1210 > n, but the code adds both 1184 and 1210 to the set. That would include 1210 which is > n, violating the spec \"from 1 to a specified number\". So there is a logic error: inclusion of numbers beyond n. So even if we correct the function name, the function might not pass all test cases if n is smaller than some amicable partner but greater than the smaller one. \n\nBut the verifier only shows a NameError, so the test didn't even get to run the logic. So for the task, we should point out the function name mismatch, then provide corrected solution that also handles the bound correctly. But the instruction says: \"Loigiai cua hoc sinh [provided] ... Kết quả kiểm tra: NameError ... Nhiệm vụ: 1. Chỉ ra CHÍNH XÁC bước suy luận / dòng code nào sai ... 2. Nguyên nhân gốc ... 3. Đưa ra lời giải đã sửa\". So we need to identify the exact error line that causes the immediate error: the function definition line has the wrong name. And then provide correct