clude both numbers. So we need to include both amicable numbers, if both are <=n. If only the smaller is <=n, include that smaller. So we need to check if b <= n, then add both? Actually we can add a when we find a pair where a is the smaller (b > a) and both are amicable. But we also need to add b if b <= n. So we could do: if b > a and d(b) == a: if b <= n: add both a and b to set, else only a. This ensures we don't miss the larger partner when it is within range. But as we iterate a, we will also later get to a = b and then it won't satisfy b > a because b (the partner) is now a (the smaller). So to avoid double counting, we use a set. Alternatively, we can iterate a from 2 to n, compute b, and if b > a and d(b) == a, then add a and if b <= n also add b to a set, then sum the set. That's similar to the student's original approach but corrected for bound. So we'll use a set.\n\nSo final code:\ndef amicable_numbers_sum(n):\n    def divisors_sum(x):\n        return sum(i for i in range(1, x) if x % i == 0)\n    amicable_set = set()\n    for a in range(2, n + 1):\n        b = divisors_sum(a)\n        if b > a and divisors_sum(b) == a:\n            amicable_set.add(a)\n            if b <= n:\n                amicable_set.add(b)\n    return sum(amicable_set)\n\nThis correctly includes both numbers if they are <= n, and only the smaller if partner > n. For n=999, a=220, b=284 <=999 so add both; a=284 later, b=220, condition b>a false, nothing added. So sum=504, matches expected. Good.\n\nThus, the error_location: the line `def sum_amicable_numbers(n):` has wrong function name. The immediate cause is the NameError. But the logic also has a potential bug with numbers > n, but the verifier didn't get to test it due to NameError. I should mention both the immediate error and the logical error, but the task asks for the exact error that caused the traceback: NameError due to function name mismatch. The student's code works for the specific test (n=999) but might fail for other n due to bound issue. However, the instruction says: \"Chỉ ra CHÍNH XÁC bước suy luận / dòng code nào sai (không đoán chung chung, không nói \"có thể sai ở đâu đó\").\" The error that caused the failure in the verifier is the function name. So I'll point to the function definition line. The error reason: the function is named `sum_amicable_numbers` while the test expects `amicable_numbers_sum`. That is the root cause of the NameError. Then provide corrected solution with proper name and logic fix.\n\nNow, format JSON. Keys: error_location, error_reason, corrected_solution. Error_location should be specific, like: \"Dòng def sum_amicable_numbers(n):\". Error_reason: \"Hàm được đặt tên sai: sum_amicable_numbers thay vì amicable_numbers_sum, dẫn đến lỗi NameError khi chương trình kiểm tra gọi amicable_numbers_sum.\" Then corrected_solution: raw Python code. I'll output the code as a string, without markdown.\n\nI will include the fixed code with both adjustments.\n\nLet's craft the JSON.\n</thinking>\n\n### Phát hiện lỗi\nDòng đầu tiên trong định nghĩa hàm: `def sum_amicable_numbers(n):`\n\n### Nguyên nhân\nTên hàm bị đặt sai. Đề bài yêu cầu viết hàm có tên `amicable_numbers_sum`, nhưng học sinh lại đặt tên hàm là `sum_amicable_numbers`. Điều này khiến chương trình kiểm tra không tìm thấy hàm `amicable_numbers_sum` và phát sinh lỗi NameError.\n\n### Sửa lại\ndef amicable_numbers_sum(n):\n    def divisors_sum(x):\n        return sum(i for i in range(1, x) if x % i == 0)\n    \n    amicable_set = set()\n    for a in range(2, n + 1):\n        b = divisors_sum(a)\n        if b > a and divisors_sum(b) == a:\n            amicable_set.add(a)\n            if b <= n:\n                amicable_set.add(b)\n    \n    return sum(amicable_set)"}]}
